{0 Getting started}

@author Etienne Millon

The goal of this tutorial is to present what a ctypes project looks like.

It assumes some familiarity with [opam] and [dune].

We are going to consider 2 functions from the standard C library and make them
available as an OCaml library,

- [double tgamma(double x)], corresponding to {m \Gamma(x)}
  ({{:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamma_function}Gamma function});
- [double gamma(double x)], corresponding to {m \log(\Gamma(x))}.

{1 Preparing the environment}

First, create a local switch and install [ctypes]:

{@shell[
mkdir ctypes-tutorial
cd ctypes-tutorial
opam switch create ./ 5.3.0
opam install ctypes
]}

{1 Creating the project}

{2 Boilerplate}

We need to create some files to set up [dune] and [ctypes].

It might seem seem a lot, but this is a one-time setup, independently of the
number of bound functions.

If you are curious about how it works, this is explained in
{{!page-how_stub_generation_works}How stub generation works}.

{3 [dune-project]}

{@dune[
(lang dune 3.17)
]}

This marks the root of the dune project and what rule versions to use.

{3 [dune]}

{@dune[
(library
 (name bindings)
 (modules bindings)
 (libraries ctypes))

(executable
 (name generator)
 (modules generator)
 (libraries ctypes.stubs bindings))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  generated.ml
  (run ./generator.exe ml)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  generated_stubs.c
  (run ./generator.exe c)))

(library
 (name generated)
 (modules generated)
 (libraries ctypes.stubs)
 (foreign_stubs
  (language c)
  (names generated_stubs)))

(library
  (name gamma)
  (modules gamma)
  (libraries bindings generated))

(executable
 (name demo)
 (modules demo)
 (libraries gamma))
]}

{3 [gamma.ml]}

{[
include Bindings.Generate(Generated)
]}

{3 [generator.ml]}

{[
let () =
  let prefix = "gamma" in
  match Sys.argv.(1) with
  | "ml" ->
      Cstubs.write_ml Format.std_formatter ~prefix (module Bindings.Generate)
  | "c" ->
      print_endline "#include <math.h>";
      Cstubs.write_c Format.std_formatter ~prefix (module Bindings.Generate)
  | _ -> assert false
]}

{2 [bindings.ml]}

{[
module Generate (F : Ctypes.FOREIGN) = struct
  open Ctypes
  open F

  let tgamma = foreign "tgamma" (double @-> returning double)
end
]}

This module contains OCaml definitions representing the C function we're binding.

{2 [demo.ml]}

{[
let () =
  Printf.printf "x  tgamma(x)\n\n";
  for i = 1 to 8 do
    let x = Float.of_int i in
    Printf.printf "%d %10g\n" i (Gamma.tgamma x)
  done
]}

This executable calls [Gamma.tgamma], an OCaml function with type [float ->
float], with inputs [1.0] to [8.0].

{1 Build and run}

Build and run the demo program:

{v
dune exec ./demo.exe
v}

It displays the values of the Gamma function, corresponding to factorial numbers:

{v
x  tgamma(x)

1          1
2          1
3          2
4          6
5         24
6        120
7        720
8       5040
v}

{1 Modify}

Open [bindings.ml] and add the definition of the [gamma] function which has the
same signature:

{[
module Generate (F : Ctypes.FOREIGN) = struct
  open Ctypes
  open F

  let tgamma = foreign "tgamma" (double @-> returning double)
  let gamma = foreign "gamma" (double @-> returning double)
  (* This line has been added *)
end
]}

And update [demo.ml] to call this function too:

{[
let () =
  Printf.printf "x  tgamma(x)   gamma(x)\n\n";
  (* Added:                  ^^^^^^^^^^^ *)
  for i = 1 to 8 do
    let x = Float.of_int i in
    Printf.printf "%d %10g %10.3f\n" i (Gamma.tgamma x) (Gamma.gamma x)
    (* Added:             ^^^^^^^                      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ *)
  done
]}

Build and run the updated version:

{v
dune exec ./demo.exe
v}

It outputs:

{v
x  tgamma(x)   gamma(x)             

1          1      0.000
2          1      0.000
3          2      0.693
4          6      1.792
5         24      3.178
6        120      4.787
7        720      6.579
8       5040      8.525
v}

{1 Conclusion}

We've created a small library that exposes 2 functions from the standard C
library, without writing any C. There was a one-time cost to set this up, but
once it is paid, changes are simple.

In the particular case of [float -> float] function, this is overcomplicated,
but this makes more interesting use cases possible.

{1 What to do next}

You might be interested in:

- continuing with {{!page-tutorial}another tutorial that goes through more
  [ctypes] features}
- learning {{!page-how_stub_generation_works}How stub generation works}
- {{!page-how_to_use_ctypes_in_a_dune_project}applying this to an existing dune project}
